INT. SCRIVENER'S SHOP - FLORENCE - 1503 - DAY

A cramped room lined with ledgers and inkpots. A high slanted desk by the window. Sunlight cuts a gold bar across the floorboards. Dust motes turn.

A MAN in a stained smock bends over a vellum sheet, quill scratching. He is thin, quick, and does not look up when the bell above the door jingles.

A woman enters. Dark cloak, veil. She closes the door softly and stands with her hands clasped.

WOMAN
I was told you write letters for those who cannot.

MAN
I write for those who will not.

WOMAN
That is a distinction.

MAN
It is the whole of my profession.

He lifts his head. Studies her. She does not raise the veil.

MAN (CONT'D)
Your name?

WOMAN
Not yet.

MAN
Then I cannot write you a letter.

WOMAN
A letter does not need my name. Only his.

MAN
Whose?

She crosses to the desk and lays a ring on the vellum. A small gold band, set with a dark stone. He picks it up. Turns it in the light. His quill stops.

MAN (CONT'D)
(quietly)
Messer Giuliano.

WOMAN
You know him.

MAN
Everyone knows him.

She lifts her veil at last. A young face, pale, composed. Her eyes do not leave his.

WOMAN
Then you know why I need a letter that will arrive after I am gone.

The scrivener sets down the ring. Dips his quill. Begins to write.

SCRIVENER
(without looking up)
Dictate, Madonna. I am listening.

Outside, a church bell tolls the hour. She draws a breath, and begins.
